WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:05.840
When you think about the greatest inventors&nbsp;
of all time, there are a few names that come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:16.240
mind. Henry Ford. The Wright brothers. Thomas Edison.&nbsp;
But there's one name that is not as recognizable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.280
When you plug your phone in, turn on the&nbsp;
lights, or use the refrigerator, you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:29.840
Nikola Tesla to thank. This is the story of the&nbsp;
forgotten genius and the story begins at the end.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:37.230
On January 7, 1943 a maid working at the New&nbsp;
Yorker Hotel walked into room 3327, where she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:42.510
found the body of an 86--year-old man who&nbsp;
called the hotel home for the past decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:48.830
Tesla died alone and broke. He lived off a diet&nbsp;
of warm milk and crackers and was obsessed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:55.070
feeding the pigeons outside. One of the greatest&nbsp;
inventors of all time faded into obscurity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:00.190
died penniless. There is a reason why this&nbsp;
happened which will become clear by the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:06.716
of this story. Tesla was born in the town of&nbsp;
Smiljan in present-day Croatia on July 10, 1856.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.716 --> 00:01:14.067
He was born during a lightning storm. According to&nbsp;
family legend, the midwife said halfway through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:20.227
birth: this child will be a child of darkness to&nbsp;
which his mother replied, no, he will be a child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.227 --> 00:01:26.547
of light. Little did she know how prophetic those&nbsp;
words would be. When Tesla was five he witnessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.547 --> 00:01:31.907
his older brother fall from a horse and later&nbsp;
die. This would haunt him for the rest of his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.907 --> 00:01:37.507
As a child, he began seeing visions accompanied by&nbsp;
flashes of light, confusing what was real and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.507 --> 00:01:43.267
was imaginary. This never went away. The vision&nbsp;
spurred his ability to conceive inventions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:49.027
his head in such detail that he didn't even need&nbsp;
to draw them out. He explained how the designs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.027 --> 00:01:55.827
were perfected in his mind in an article in 1919.&nbsp;
"Invariably, my device works as I conceived that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.827 --> 00:02:01.507
it should and the experiment comes out exactly&nbsp;
as I planned it. In 20 years there has not been&nbsp;

00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:08.307
a single exception." Tesla credits his mom for his&nbsp;
interest in invention. Đuka Mandić invented small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.307 --> 00:02:14.387
household appliances in her spare time. She had an&nbsp;
eidetic memory - the ability to recall an image from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.387 --> 00:02:20.227
memory with high precision and she passed this on&nbsp;
to her son. Tesla's father was a priest and wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:25.987
him to become one too but Tesla was interested in&nbsp;
engineering. When he contracted cholera as a teen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.987 --> 00:02:30.787
and nearly died, his father promised to send&nbsp;
him to engineering school if he survived and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.787 --> 00:02:36.387
miraculously, he did. He went to study in Austria&nbsp;
at the Technical College of Graz where he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:41.987
said to have worked from 3 am until 11 pm&nbsp;
every day. Professors were worried that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.987 --> 00:02:47.827
would die from exhaustion. Tesla had a beautiful&nbsp;
mind. He could perform calculus in his head and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:52.387
spoke eight languages. He was a good student&nbsp;
at the start but would not finish school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:58.867
He dropped out after becoming addicted to gambling&nbsp;
and cut ties with his family so they wouldn't find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:03.747
out. His friends didn't know what happened to&nbsp;
him either. They thought he drowned in a river.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.747 --> 00:03:08.455
Tesla moved around Europe and eventually ended&nbsp;
up in Budapest working as an electrician at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.455 --> 00:03:13.577
telephone company. While walking around a park&nbsp;
in the city one day, he had an epiphany about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.577 --> 00:03:19.657
developing a new way of generating electricity&nbsp;
using alternating current. It would be his greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:25.897
invention that would change the world. I'll&nbsp;
explain more about AC a little later. In 1882,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.897 --> 00:03:31.417
he settled in Paris to work for the French branch&nbsp;
of Thomas Edison's electric company. He started off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.417 --> 00:03:35.337
installing indoor lighting but the managers&nbsp;
noticed his talents and had him doing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.337 --> 00:03:41.017
complicated work, designing and building dynamos&nbsp;
and motors. He was soon traveling throughout Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.017 --> 00:03:47.337
fixing problems at other Edison branches. Two&nbsp;
years later, in 1884, Tesla's manager offered him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.337 --> 00:03:53.017
a job at Edison Machine Works in New York City.
He agreed and arrived in America with only four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:58.457
cents in his pocket because his money was stolen&nbsp;
on the boat ride over. Tesla initially had a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.457 --> 00:04:03.897
impression of Edison. Edison was also impressed by&nbsp;
Tesla, later saying: "I have had many hard-working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.897 --> 00:04:09.897
assistants but you take the cake." This mutual&nbsp;
admiration didn't last. They would become bitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.897 --> 00:04:15.977
rivals. The two men disagreed over how electricity&nbsp;
should be contained and delivered. Edison preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.977 --> 00:04:21.257
direct current which is a system where the&nbsp;
electric charge only flows in one direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.257 --> 00:04:26.937
Tesla was a fan of alternating current in which&nbsp;
the electric charge changes direction periodically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.937 --> 00:04:33.311
Changing directions is crucial to maintaining a&nbsp;
steady supply of electricity because it does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.311 --> 00:04:38.911
overpower outlets. This means it can provide&nbsp;
more power and transmit power over longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:45.471
distances. It's the reason AC powers our homes&nbsp;
and other large appliances whereas DC powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.471 --> 00:04:51.311
smaller items like flashlights. But Edison didn't&nbsp;
care about AC because it could have hurt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.311 --> 00:04:57.391
sales of direct current since he owned all the&nbsp;
patents for DC. According to Tesla, a manager at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.391 --> 00:05:03.471
Edison's company offered him a $50,000 bonus if&nbsp;
he could improve some machines that ran on DC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.471 --> 00:05:09.278
When he did, the manager refused to pay up. Another&nbsp;
account of the story has Edison telling Tesla:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.278 --> 00:05:15.038
"You don't understand our American humor." Regardless&nbsp;
of how it played out, Tesla quit and set off to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.038 --> 00:05:20.718
form his own electric company the following year&nbsp;
in 1885. But his investors showed little interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.718 --> 00:05:25.198
and decided to take the company and all of&nbsp;
Tesla's patents which they could do because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.198 --> 00:05:30.398
Tesla had assigned the patents to the company in&nbsp;
exchange for stock which was now worthless. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.398 --> 00:05:36.718
losing his company, Tesla had to take a job digging&nbsp;
ditches for two dollars a day just to survive. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.718 --> 00:05:44.238
his fortunes would change. In 1887, Tesla invented&nbsp;
an induction motor that ran on alternating current.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.238 --> 00:05:49.438
The motor was the most efficient way to convert&nbsp;
electricity to mechanical power. Aversion of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.438 --> 00:05:54.638
powers Tesla's vehicles which took its name from&nbsp;
the inventor. He patented the motor and showed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.638 --> 00:05:59.598
off the following year at the American Institute&nbsp;
of Electrical Engineers that caught the attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.598 --> 00:06:05.198
of George Westinghouse, a major player in the&nbsp;
electric market who realized Tesla's AC motor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:09.758
might just be what he needed to complete his&nbsp;
alternating current system and compete against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.758 --> 00:06:14.958
Edison's DC system. So Tesla licensed the&nbsp;
patents for the AC motor to Westinghouse for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.958 --> 00:06:20.798
$60,000 and also received stock and&nbsp;
royalties. Westinghouse hired him as a consultant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.798 --> 00:06:25.598
for $2,000 a month which is the&nbsp;
equivalent of over $50,000 a month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.598 --> 00:06:31.518
today. The war of the currents began. Edison tried&nbsp;
hard to try to discredit Westinghouse and Tesla.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.518 --> 00:06:37.518
He secretly financed the electric chair that used&nbsp;
alternating current to prove how dangerous AC was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.518 --> 00:06:43.918
Edison's company also publicly tortured animals&nbsp;
to prove its point. In 1903, they electrocuted a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.918 --> 00:06:49.518
circus elephant named Topsy and produced a film&nbsp;
about it called Electrocuting an Elephant. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.518 --> 00:06:54.798
Edison's schemes, good things were happening for&nbsp;
Westinghouse and Tesla. They underbid Edison and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.798 --> 00:06:59.518
his newly formed company General Electric to&nbsp;
illuminate the World's Colombian Exposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:05.758
in Chicago in 1893. The first all-electric fair&nbsp;
celebrated the 400th anniversary of Christopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:12.798
Columbus's discovery of America. It was clear to&nbsp;
the 27 million people who attended that AC would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.798 --> 00:07:17.998
power the future. Their success continued when&nbsp;
they beat out Edison's General Electric again to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.998 --> 00:07:23.518
build the world's first alternating current power&nbsp;
plant in Niagara Falls. The hydroelectric power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.518 --> 00:07:28.718
station was a massive success and helped light up&nbsp;
Buffalo, New York. The building of the plant also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.718 --> 00:07:34.558
meant Tesla became a pioneer in renewable energy.&nbsp;
His statue can be found at Niagara Falls today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.558 --> 00:07:40.360
Westinghouse and Tesla won the war of the&nbsp;
currents and direct current was being phased out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:47.042
But there were problems. Westinghouse's company&nbsp;
was running out of money and eventually went $10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.042 --> 00:07:52.802
million into debt. In 1897, he went to Tesla&nbsp;
and asked if his royalties could be reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.802 --> 00:07:58.322
in a desperate attempt to save the company. Tesla&nbsp;
was so compelled by compassion for his friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.322 --> 00:08:04.604
that he ripped up his contract. He was grateful to&nbsp;
Westinghouse for believing in him when no one else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.604 --> 00:08:10.684
would. Tesla willingly walked away from $12 million&nbsp;
in royalties which in today's terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.684 --> 00:08:16.444
would be worth over $300 million. Had he held on&nbsp;
to those royalties over time, he would have likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.444 --> 00:08:21.815
become the wealthiest person on the planet and the&nbsp;
first person with a billion dollar net worth. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.815 --> 00:08:27.655
act of compassion for his friend of tearing up his&nbsp;
contract saved Westinghouse. In return, Westinghouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.655 --> 00:08:33.474
paid Tesla $216,000 for the rights to use as ac patents forever. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.474 --> 00:08:38.674
is the equivalent of about $60 million
today. With that money, Tesla became financially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.674 --> 00:08:43.794
independent and set up a series of laboratories in&nbsp;
New York for new projects where he was visited by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.794 --> 00:08:49.154
the rich and famous, including his close friend and&nbsp;
one of the greatest American writers of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.154 --> 00:08:55.314
Mark Twain. This was his period of many inventions.&nbsp;
He held over 300 patents in his lifetime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.314 --> 00:09:01.394
He created an early version of neon lighting, the&nbsp;
tesla turbine - a bladeless turbine for vehicles. &nbsp;

00:09:01.434 --> 00:09:07.594
He pioneered x-ray technology by experimenting&nbsp;
with radiation. This is an x-ray of his own hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.594 --> 00:09:13.434
Another stand-out invention was one of the first&nbsp;
remote controls. In 1898, he controlled a miniature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.434 --> 00:09:18.554
boat at Madison Square Garden in New York. It was&nbsp;
so far ahead of its time that the crowd thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.554 --> 00:09:24.554
he was using magic to make it move. That would be&nbsp;
the ancestor to today's remote-controlled drones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:31.094
One of his most well-known inventions is the Tesla&nbsp;
coil - a device that can produce large amounts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.094 --> 00:09:36.614
high voltage electricity. Because of the coils,
he discovered he could send and receive powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.614 --> 00:09:41.795
radio signals when they resonated at the same&nbsp;
frequency. Tesla was getting ready to broadcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.795 --> 00:09:50.115
his first radio signal but disaster struck. A&nbsp;
fire destroyed his lab in 1895. He lost years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.115 --> 00:09:56.195
of research and equipment. Tesla didn't apply for&nbsp;
a patent for the radio until two years later. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.195 --> 00:10:02.515
fire would be the turning point in his life that&nbsp;
led to a downhill spiral. At the same time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.515 --> 00:10:08.275
he was working on radio, an Italian entrepreneur,&nbsp;
Guglielmo Marconi, was also working on the radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.275 --> 00:10:13.795
in England. He tried to acquire patent rights in the&nbsp;
US but was turned down because it was too similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.795 --> 00:10:20.115
to Tesla's. However, things changed when Marconi was&nbsp;
able to send the world's first transatlantic radio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.115 --> 00:10:27.635
message in 1901 using 17 of Tesla's patents. Edison&nbsp;
then threw his financial support behind Marconi.

00:10:27.635 --> 00:10:34.515
Tesla had no problem with Marconi's achievements&nbsp;
but in 1904, the US Patent Office suddenly changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.515 --> 00:10:39.075
its mind and awarded Marconi a patent for&nbsp;
the invention the radio. There has never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.075 --> 00:10:43.875
been a reason given for this decision but the&nbsp;
powerful financial backing Marconi received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.875 --> 00:10:50.355
could explain it. Marconi went on to win the Nobel&nbsp;
Prize in Physics in 1911 which was only possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.355 --> 00:10:56.755
due to Tesla's work. Tesla was furious and sued&nbsp;
Marconi. The case dragged on in court for years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.755 --> 00:11:02.755
was only settled in Tesla's favor after his death.&nbsp;
That radio incident negatively impacted the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.755 --> 00:11:08.435
of Tesla's career. For example, Tesla was obsessed&nbsp;
with bringing wireless communication to the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.435 --> 00:11:14.275
and built a huge wireless transmission station in&nbsp;
Long Island, New York called Wardenclyffe Tower. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.275 --> 00:11:19.875
imagined a world where we could send and receive&nbsp;
messages wirelessly. He was, again, well ahead of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.875 --> 00:11:25.075
time. But financial backers did not have enough&nbsp;
faith in his project. They pulled out and banked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.075 --> 00:11:31.315
on Marconi's radio invention instead. This left&nbsp;
Tesla in financial ruin. He had no choice but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.315 --> 00:11:37.850
abandon his dream project in 1905 and eventually&nbsp;
lost Wardenclyffe Tower to foreclosure. Tesla's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:44.330
mental health deteriorated. He lived his last&nbsp;
decade in the New Yorker Hotel beginning in 1933.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:50.810
Westinghouse Corporation hired him as a consultant&nbsp;
and paid for his room. He lived rent-free but died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:57.370
in debt. So why did one of the greatest inventors&nbsp;
of all time fade into obscurity and die penniless?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:02.090
You could say T esla was unlucky at times like&nbsp;
when the fire burned down his New York lab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:07.416
But the main reason is because Tesla was not&nbsp;
a capitalist. He made decisions that those with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.416 --> 00:12:12.936
more business acumen would not have made such as&nbsp;
giving up his royalties for the AC motor. He wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.936 --> 00:12:18.536
concerned about money. He was concerned about the&nbsp;
pursuit of science for the betterment of humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.536 --> 00:12:24.616
He wanted to change the world and he did. Thanks&nbsp;
in part to Elon Musk's company, people are starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.616 --> 00:12:28.856
to learn more about the man who inspired&nbsp;
the company, a man whose inventions would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.856 --> 00:12:35.256
power our entire planet. It's because of Tesla&nbsp;
that modern society functions the way it does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.256 --> 00:12:40.172
Tesla's mother called him a child of light&nbsp;
and she was quite right. 

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:44.568
Thanks for watching the story of Nikola Tesla, I hope you&nbsp;
enjoyed it. 

